      <div class="Section3"><a name="_cultural_naming_profiles" id="_cultural_naming_profiles"></a>
        <h1 class="Annex"><b>Annex B</b><br/>(informative)<br/><br/><b>Cultural naming profiles</b></h1>
        <div><a name="_afghan" id="_afghan"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.1 Afghan</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_albanian" id="_albanian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.2 Albanian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_arab_muslim" id="_arab_muslim"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.3 Arab-Muslim</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_bangladeshi" id="_bangladeshi"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.4 Bangladeshi</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_bosnian_serbian_croats" id="_bosnian_serbian_croats"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.5 Bosnian, Serbian, Croats</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_brazilian" id="_brazilian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.6 Brazilian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_bulgarian" id="_bulgarian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.7 Bulgarian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_chinese" id="_chinese"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.8 Chinese</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_8ef161a4-b7ba-45cb-a94a-036687cf8204" id="_8ef161a4-b7ba-45cb-a94a-036687cf8204"></a>family name + given name(s)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_d215b3b7-d20e-4bfd-a3ab-a0791738aa8d" id="_d215b3b7-d20e-4bfd-a3ab-a0791738aa8d"></a>Chinese personal names are names used by those from mainland China, Hong Kong,
Macau, Taiwan, and the Chinese diaspora overseas.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_9d925706-5dfb-49c4-b337-47a71f14d1fc" id="_9d925706-5dfb-49c4-b337-47a71f14d1fc"></a>Chinese names consist of a family name (&#x59D3;, x&#xEC;ng), followed by
a given name (&#x540D;, m&#xED;ng).</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_284c8791-a692-49f6-b94d-998931a3f9e8" id="_284c8791-a692-49f6-b94d-998931a3f9e8"></a>Some people also utilize a &#x201C;courtesy name&#x201D; or &#x201C;style name&#x201D; (&#x5B57;, z&#xEC;),
by which they were known among those outside their
family and closest friends.</p></div>
        <div><a name="_colombian" id="_colombian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.9 Colombian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_czech" id="_czech"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.10 Czech</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_english" id="_english"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.11 English</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_d1709412-4f84-43c7-b8c5-65af057425ae" id="_d1709412-4f84-43c7-b8c5-65af057425ae"></a>given name(s) (+ optional family name)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_16167ac0-f8e0-4c99-be16-0730b671823e" id="_16167ac0-f8e0-4c99-be16-0730b671823e"></a>English names are names used in, or originating in, England. In England as
elsewhere in the English-speaking world, a complete name usually consists of a
given name, commonly referred to as a first name or Christian name, and a (most
commonly patrilineal) family name or surname, also referred to as a last name.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_13307f23-fa87-46fa-b081-59c4dd91465e" id="_13307f23-fa87-46fa-b081-59c4dd91465e"></a>There can be several given names, some of these being often referred to as a
second name, or middle name(s).</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_7a5e27aa-1b9d-46a8-8aa2-d4ea3e28efd6" id="_7a5e27aa-1b9d-46a8-8aa2-d4ea3e28efd6"></a>Double-barrelled names, compound surnames in English feature two words,
are often joined by a hyphen or hyphens.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_ef6d4a1c-ea69-41a0-a0e6-a17d901650c0" id="_ef6d4a1c-ea69-41a0-a0e6-a17d901650c0"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Henry Hepburne-Scott</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_c6496064-5a2d-4b7f-a782-44fb4cd3e984" id="_c6496064-5a2d-4b7f-a782-44fb4cd3e984"></a>Some families having as many as
three or four words making up their surname.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_05f5acc7-57ea-438e-ac78-04dde18bd35e" id="_05f5acc7-57ea-438e-ac78-04dde18bd35e"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Charles Hepburn-Stuart-Forbes-Trefusis, 21st Baron Clinton.</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_323efe7d-7e7c-485b-9027-88b9a484ce16" id="_323efe7d-7e7c-485b-9027-88b9a484ce16"></a>It is not unusual for compound surnames to be composed of separate words
not linked by a hyphen.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_59763ca3-22d2-49d6-a8a0-9fe1f6e52ff2" id="_59763ca3-22d2-49d6-a8a0-9fe1f6e52ff2"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Iain Duncan Smith has a surname of &#x201C;Duncan Smith&#x201D;.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_french" id="_french"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.12 French</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_24dc8d3a-652f-4716-b86d-faa138b0c8c4" id="_24dc8d3a-652f-4716-b86d-faa138b0c8c4"></a>given name(s) (+ optional family name)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_d9dd18b2-6da5-46ff-8440-3a99ebdd16e1" id="_d9dd18b2-6da5-46ff-8440-3a99ebdd16e1"></a>French people have one, two or more given names (first names). One of them
is used in daily life.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_4743a5e4-abe8-41b3-99c9-ecd632dd0870" id="_4743a5e4-abe8-41b3-99c9-ecd632dd0870"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x201C;Jacques Chirac&#x201D; instead of officially of Jacques Ren&#xE9; Chirac.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_3237f81a-638f-4f3a-adab-cde52d5a212a" id="_3237f81a-638f-4f3a-adab-cde52d5a212a"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x201C;Philippe P&#xE9;tain&#x201D; instead of officially of Henri Philippe P&#xE9;tain,
because Philippe was the given name that he used in daily
life.</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_213738b0-19e8-45e3-acd7-cb35ab731595" id="_213738b0-19e8-45e3-acd7-cb35ab731595"></a>Middle names in the English sense do not exist, and middle initials are
never used for second or further given names.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_517fc758-02b8-4cbd-931a-46e120b9c501" id="_517fc758-02b8-4cbd-931a-46e120b9c501"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Claude All&#xE8;gre may be cited as &#x201C;Claude J. All&#xE8;gre&#x201D; in English-speaking scientific publications, but not in French ones.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_german" id="_german"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.13 German</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_aeff57f3-0ee1-4edc-b510-a7639d08df8e" id="_aeff57f3-0ee1-4edc-b510-a7639d08df8e"></a>given name(s) (+ optional family name)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_ad9f220a-44f6-4c57-b057-04ac3d51d96f" id="_ad9f220a-44f6-4c57-b057-04ac3d51d96f"></a>Personal names in German-speaking Europe consist of one or several given names
(Vorname, plural Vornamen) and a surname (Nachname, Familienname). The Vorname
is usually gender-specific.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_c5678636-f79c-4bc5-aa1a-fc775d26fab8" id="_c5678636-f79c-4bc5-aa1a-fc775d26fab8"></a>Typical order of &#x201C;given name, surname&#x201D;.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_917d26e9-14ae-42f9-a265-3bcb7eaf785f" id="_917d26e9-14ae-42f9-a265-3bcb7eaf785f"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Otto Eduard Leopold F&#xFC;rst von Bismarck.</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_300faa69-9415-447d-83f2-6853c71bbc1b" id="_300faa69-9415-447d-83f2-6853c71bbc1b"></a>The Vorname (in English forename) is usually given to a child by the parents
shortly after birth. It is common to give a child several Vornamen (forenames).</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_042053b0-9ba7-4587-ab5f-81985b2b073c" id="_042053b0-9ba7-4587-ab5f-81985b2b073c"></a>The Rufname (&#x201C;appellation name&#x201D; or &#x201C;call name&#x201D;) is the given name
intended for everyday use, and is often underlined on official documents.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_fdd9d137-51bb-41e6-bd8d-abb2740eaffc" id="_fdd9d137-51bb-41e6-bd8d-abb2740eaffc"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>The Rufname of the mathematician Amalie Emmy Noether is &#x201C;Emmy&#x201D;.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_greek" id="_greek"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.14 Greek</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_hindu" id="_hindu"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.15 Hindu</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_hungarian" id="_hungarian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.16 Hungarian</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_f4209a7e-2f2e-4b02-af84-86d7bfc2eb99" id="_f4209a7e-2f2e-4b02-af84-86d7bfc2eb99"></a>family name(s) + given name(s)</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_8d2b2fee-fe73-4ac2-bcb4-f7e92654a3c9" id="_8d2b2fee-fe73-4ac2-bcb4-f7e92654a3c9"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>KOVA&#x301;CS Ja&#x301;nos</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_9e19f429-6cf9-4d49-948a-2e3c4c1a5da5" id="_9e19f429-6cf9-4d49-948a-2e3c4c1a5da5"></a>If there is more than one given name, they come in order of importance
after the family name:</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_2dd877df-f0c2-4bea-9009-d90fc2741cc7" id="_2dd877df-f0c2-4bea-9009-d90fc2741cc7"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>KOVA&#x301;CS Ja&#x301;nos Alada&#x301;r.</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_41bd1350-daad-49c6-84e6-3d1c5c2e7523" id="_41bd1350-daad-49c6-84e6-3d1c5c2e7523"></a>There can be more than one family name, which can be written fully or abbreviated
into initials:</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_557c32bc-7c6f-48e6-8de0-73ddb2c083f9" id="_557c32bc-7c6f-48e6-8de0-73ddb2c083f9"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>SEGESDY KOVA&#x301;CS Ja&#x301;nos</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_f2ce07ce-ffdf-4e2b-9ea5-32634d4ea8cc" id="_f2ce07ce-ffdf-4e2b-9ea5-32634d4ea8cc"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  4</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>SEGESDY-KOVA&#x301;CS Ja&#x301;nos</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_b219c827-0de1-4516-b2a5-497d12bd98a6" id="_b219c827-0de1-4516-b2a5-497d12bd98a6"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  5</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>S.KOVA&#x301;CS Ja&#x301;nos</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_1ce0147e-36a7-4a4f-9560-471a3711fb9d" id="_1ce0147e-36a7-4a4f-9560-471a3711fb9d"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  6</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>SEGESDY K. Ja&#x301;nos</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_icelandic" id="_icelandic"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.17 Icelandic</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_aacf43a7-2ad1-42af-a9f1-afd1a1ee9fef" id="_aacf43a7-2ad1-42af-a9f1-afd1a1ee9fef"></a>given name(s) + (patronymic or matronymic) (+ optional genonymic) (+ optional family name)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_1077e7f6-5851-433d-8e32-8bc44e7b6a70" id="_1077e7f6-5851-433d-8e32-8bc44e7b6a70"></a>Icelandic names differ from most current Western family name systems by being
patronymic or occasionally matronymic: they indicate the father (or mother) of
the child and not the historic family lineage.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_d74caff0-a290-4f82-83bc-6024079eb49e" id="_d74caff0-a290-4f82-83bc-6024079eb49e"></a>Iceland&#x2019;s traditional name system was formerly used by all Nordic countries
except partly Finland.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_3a757de6-7991-46c1-b4dc-7be6798b6e0c" id="_3a757de6-7991-46c1-b4dc-7be6798b6e0c"></a>The Icelandic system is not based on family names
(although some people do have family names and might use both systems).
Generally, a person&#x2019;s second name indicates the first name of their father
(patronymic) or in some cases mother (matronymic). According to Icelandic
naming tradition, second names end in -son or -d&#xF3;ttir with few exceptions.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_762f3831-862e-4ea4-abb4-6df37a801eef" id="_762f3831-862e-4ea4-abb4-6df37a801eef"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>J&#xF3;hanna Sigur&#xF0;ard&#xF3;ttir. &#x201C;J&#xF3;hanna, daughter of Sigur&#xF0;ur&#x201D;.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_85bd6a18-711b-47b3-8278-9d0eb3a2c3a3" id="_85bd6a18-711b-47b3-8278-9d0eb3a2c3a3"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Matronymic. Hei&#xF0;ar Helguson, &#x201C;Helguson, son of Helga&#x201D;.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_6962c924-a7d5-4bf0-9b45-a82419868ddf" id="_6962c924-a7d5-4bf0-9b45-a82419868ddf"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#xD6;rvar &#xDE;&#xF3;reyjarson Sm&#xE1;rason. (&#xD6;rvar, son of &#xDE;&#xF3;rey, son of Sm&#xE1;ri)</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_1eeceefd-2985-417a-9b87-82bcc9eb0b03" id="_1eeceefd-2985-417a-9b87-82bcc9eb0b03"></a>Some family names do exist in Iceland, most commonly adaptations from last name
patronyms Icelanders took up when living abroad.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_5969a970-8931-44b0-884f-0188b95aaf06" id="_5969a970-8931-44b0-884f-0188b95aaf06"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  4</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Geir Haarde, Ei&#xF0;ur Sm&#xE1;ri Gu&#xF0;johnsen, Anita Briem.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_c91328b5-8085-4d93-a566-9ea1369672e8" id="_c91328b5-8085-4d93-a566-9ea1369672e8"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  5</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Nobel Prize-winning author Halld&#xF3;r Laxness, born as Halld&#xF3;r Gu&#xF0;j&#xF3;nsson.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_indian" id="_indian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.18 Indian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_indonesian" id="_indonesian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.19 Indonesian</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_d034a069-eb02-428b-a43f-458a557afb04" id="_d034a069-eb02-428b-a43f-458a557afb04"></a>given name(s)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_d7f533f5-d4f2-425e-b779-10e4f4ff8a91" id="_d7f533f5-d4f2-425e-b779-10e4f4ff8a91"></a>Indonesians generally use given names without a family name.
The majority of Indonesians do not have family names. Rather,
their given names are geographically and culturally specific.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_70c828cf-9301-492b-89b7-da344ed1de88" id="_70c828cf-9301-492b-89b7-da344ed1de88"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Mulyono, the original name of Joko Widodo.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_98da3283-8963-4d91-99b9-c84e4a17bf31" id="_98da3283-8963-4d91-99b9-c84e4a17bf31"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Kusno Sosrodihardjo.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_353e7010-9044-4cbd-a97f-a2ba6e8ca455" id="_353e7010-9044-4cbd-a97f-a2ba6e8ca455"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Fransiskus Xaverius Hadi Rudyatmo</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_8e6dc0d4-5dbd-41d5-9e65-ffb74c514299" id="_8e6dc0d4-5dbd-41d5-9e65-ffb74c514299"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  4</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Sudono Salim</p></div>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_a559e81e-0cc5-4ecf-ad8f-aa268e2d305b" id="_a559e81e-0cc5-4ecf-ad8f-aa268e2d305b"></a>Some given names include a family name or a patronymic.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_3f6a310c-dc2f-428b-ac22-a220a9e34e07" id="_3f6a310c-dc2f-428b-ac22-a220a9e34e07"></a>Indonesian names usually fall into one of the following categories:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo19;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    Mononymic names
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo19;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Polynymic names without a family name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo19;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Polynymic names with a family name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo19;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    Polynymic names with patronymic
  </p>

<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_169165f9-da14-449c-b808-d0f7d9e27102" id="_169165f9-da14-449c-b808-d0f7d9e27102"></a>An Indonesian name is often mis-represented in other cultures, such as:</p>

  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo20;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpFirst">
    Netherlands: the word &#x201C;Onbekend&#x201D; (&#x201C;unknown&#x201D;) is assigned as a last name
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo20;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpMiddle">
    Germany: the one-word name is used as both given name and surname
  </p>
  <p style="mso-list:l3 level1 lfo20;" class="MsoListParagraphCxSpLast">
    US: &#x201C;Fnu&#x201D; (family name unknown) is assigned as the first or given name, &#x201C;Lnu&#x201D; (last name unknown) is assigned as the family, surname or last name,
or the name is duplicated as the surname.
  </p>
</div>
        <div><a name="_iranian" id="_iranian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.20 Iranian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_iraqi" id="_iraqi"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.21 Iraqi</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_italian" id="_italian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.22 Italian</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_c15dcf98-5477-4880-a803-101c2557d65e" id="_c15dcf98-5477-4880-a803-101c2557d65e"></a>given name(s) + family name</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_e9c86726-3bd7-4b13-9445-a80cf76f5b0c" id="_e9c86726-3bd7-4b13-9445-a80cf76f5b0c"></a>A name in Italian consists of a given name (nome) and a surname (cognome).
Surnames are normally written after given names.</p></div>
        <div><a name="_japanese" id="_japanese"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.23 Japanese</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_ab0c06d8-2a0b-4b6a-8d81-19521d8a8ab2" id="_ab0c06d8-2a0b-4b6a-8d81-19521d8a8ab2"></a>family name + given name(s)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_13f36721-f748-4cda-9e1b-ea5e22449030" id="_13f36721-f748-4cda-9e1b-ea5e22449030"></a>Japanese names usually consist of a family name (surname),
followed by a given name.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_a00bc31c-b75d-4e97-ade9-46521093510f" id="_a00bc31c-b75d-4e97-ade9-46521093510f"></a>Japanese names are usually written in kanji,
where characters are logographic, but each with a variety of
possible Japanese pronunciations. Therefore
often names cannot in general be spelled or
pronounced unless both the spelling and pronunciation are given.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_773c35eb-12c5-4586-9e58-36c7764b3b3d" id="_773c35eb-12c5-4586-9e58-36c7764b3b3d"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x5927;&#x7FD4; is traditionally pronounced &#x201C;Hiroto&#x201D;, but can also be pronounced as
&#x201C;Haruto&#x201D;, &#x201C;Yamato&#x201D;, &#x201C;Taiga&#x201D;, &#x201C;Sora&#x201D;, &#x201C;Taito&#x201D;, &#x201C;Daito&#x201D;, and
&#x201C;Masato&#x201D;.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_2021aaf4-adc9-4721-8d03-ad79eeb00019" id="_2021aaf4-adc9-4721-8d03-ad79eeb00019"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x5927;&#x548C; can be read as Daiwa or Yamato.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_34ecab0a-c730-4d95-8dfd-214635b7d199" id="_34ecab0a-c730-4d95-8dfd-214635b7d199"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x6771;&#x6D77;&#x6797; can be read as T&#x14D;kairin or Sh&#x14D;ji</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_jewish" id="_jewish"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.24 Jewish</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_65c9e476-7f1e-46e4-b943-f16ad2a00465" id="_65c9e476-7f1e-46e4-b943-f16ad2a00465"></a>given name(s) + family names</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_e5cda62c-9884-404a-9cbc-b60e9ef2dd45" id="_e5cda62c-9884-404a-9cbc-b60e9ef2dd45"></a>or</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_d446c66d-b8a1-4017-87e9-d2c68ee54205" id="_d446c66d-b8a1-4017-87e9-d2c68ee54205"></a>given name(s) + patronymic name</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_0b67626f-beac-4b6f-a26f-f0d44a651a85" id="_0b67626f-beac-4b6f-a26f-f0d44a651a85"></a>Modern Jewish names include Hebrew patronymic names as well as family names.</p></div>
        <div><a name="_korean" id="_korean"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.25 Korean</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_557498b8-3a5c-4cd7-8757-c6a725cfbd15" id="_557498b8-3a5c-4cd7-8757-c6a725cfbd15"></a>family name + given name(s)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_c8b186e2-0f43-4968-9b09-7831c46d87be" id="_c8b186e2-0f43-4968-9b09-7831c46d87be"></a>A Korean name consists of a family name followed by a given name, as used by
the Korean people in both South Korea and North Korea.</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_a8219f5a-4258-4d6a-bf3d-1a6268a75420" id="_a8219f5a-4258-4d6a-bf3d-1a6268a75420"></a>In the Korean language,
ireum or seongmyeong usually refers to the family name (seong) and given name
(myeong) together.</p></div>
        <div><a name="_kurdish" id="_kurdish"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.26 Kurdish</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_latvian" id="_latvian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.27 Latvian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_lithuanian" id="_lithuanian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.28 Lithuanian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_macedonian" id="_macedonian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.29 Macedonian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_manchu" id="_manchu"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.30 Manchu</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_e7d708ea-ead5-4b07-a1cc-4a971363f631" id="_e7d708ea-ead5-4b07-a1cc-4a971363f631"></a>given name(s) (+ optional family name)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_6d152368-cad4-48f9-b93a-dac481d31218" id="_6d152368-cad4-48f9-b93a-dac481d31218"></a>Manchus are called only by their given names.
Manchu clan names (&#x201C;hala&#x201D;) are not used with their personal names,
but occasionally a family name is used.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_edd965fd-490c-47a8-a3be-2eb208420a5c" id="_edd965fd-490c-47a8-a3be-2eb208420a5c"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Tuli&#x161;en</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_c50b8f54-d23f-4208-ba00-0c96f383551d" id="_c50b8f54-d23f-4208-ba00-0c96f383551d"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Hiowan Ye (&#x7384;&#x70E8;)</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_6c3c3b34-0661-419d-84bd-0dfead00d8ce" id="_6c3c3b34-0661-419d-84bd-0dfead00d8ce"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  3</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Aisin-Gioro Ulhicun, linguist. The family name is &#x201C;Aisin-Gioro&#x201D;.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_mongolian" id="_mongolian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.31 Mongolian</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_32846381-2cf0-4362-916f-05482a18d419" id="_32846381-2cf0-4362-916f-05482a18d419"></a>given name (+ optional clan name or family name)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_527cfecf-eb29-49a7-84b5-7b91c6205a3d" id="_527cfecf-eb29-49a7-84b5-7b91c6205a3d"></a>or</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_10ab3a43-fdc9-4409-bcc4-052bd21772bb" id="_10ab3a43-fdc9-4409-bcc4-052bd21772bb"></a>genonymic + given name</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_da4c1c2a-03c5-44d6-8d84-1b5bb24eaf86" id="_da4c1c2a-03c5-44d6-8d84-1b5bb24eaf86"></a>Mongolian names traditionally consists of only one personal name.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_bdda380d-1968-4c2e-bec1-d77889848627" id="_bdda380d-1968-4c2e-bec1-d77889848627"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x41E;&#x442;&#x440;&#x44F;&#x434;&#x44B;&#x43D; &#x413;&#x4AF;&#x43D;&#x434;&#x44D;&#x433;&#x43C;&#x430;&#x430; (&#x201C;Otryadyn G&#xFC;ndegmaa&#x201D;), a Mongolian shooter, has &#x201C;Otryadyn&#x201D; as a patronym, &#x201C;G&#xFC;ndegmaa&#x201D; as given name.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_a4527888-d6c9-4261-9319-e658209aca98" id="_a4527888-d6c9-4261-9319-e658209aca98"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x425;&#x430;&#x43B;&#x442;&#x43C;&#x430;&#x430;&#x433;&#x438;&#x439;&#x43D; &#x411;&#x430;&#x442;&#x442;&#x443;&#x43B;&#x433;&#x430; (&#x201C;Khaltmaagiin Battulga&#x201D;), President of Mongolia.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_nigerian" id="_nigerian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.32 Nigerian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_pakistani" id="_pakistani"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.33 Pakistani</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_portuguese" id="_portuguese"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.34 Portuguese</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_cca0ba2f-1647-4a0b-ae1d-6c8c73cccbfa" id="_cca0ba2f-1647-4a0b-ae1d-6c8c73cccbfa"></a>given name(s) + mother&#x2019;s patronymic name(s) + father&#x2019;s patronymic name(s)</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_21cd68c3-73e9-4f8b-b7c1-ebdd40eb8901" id="_21cd68c3-73e9-4f8b-b7c1-ebdd40eb8901"></a>Portuguese often have two personal names, although the first may at times be used on its own.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_e40cb7d2-ca82-4ee6-bdbe-98e5c007d388" id="_e40cb7d2-ca82-4ee6-bdbe-98e5c007d388"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>George Agostinho Baptista da Silva.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_0e794064-536c-4094-9cb5-144fd132fdf2" id="_0e794064-536c-4094-9cb5-144fd132fdf2"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Ant&#xF3;nio Caetano de Abreu Freire Egas Moniz, Nobel prize winner. He was the child of Fernando de Pina Rezende Abreu and Maria do Rosario de Almeida e Sousa.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_romanian" id="_romanian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.35 Romanian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_russian" id="_russian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.36 Russian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_sikh" id="_sikh"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.37 Sikh</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_sinhalese" id="_sinhalese"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.38 Sinhalese</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_slavic" id="_slavic"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.39 Slavic</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_74bc186d-7232-412c-841f-f204fdcf9b17" id="_74bc186d-7232-412c-841f-f204fdcf9b17"></a>given name + patronymic name + family name</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_7362a449-2200-4f57-8e05-50d234416bb0" id="_7362a449-2200-4f57-8e05-50d234416bb0"></a>Slavic naming applies to Russian, Ukrainian, Belorussian,
Bulgaria, Bosnia and Herzegovina, Serbia and Macedonia,
as well as some populations of Kazakhstan, Kyrgyzstan, Uzbekistan and Turkmenistan.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_44ce3d1b-b2dc-4894-a7f3-0fb3db71ce59" id="_44ce3d1b-b2dc-4894-a7f3-0fb3db71ce59"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  1</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x418;&#x432;&#x430;&#x301;&#x43D; &#x41A;&#x43E;&#x43D;&#x441;&#x442;&#x430;&#x43D;&#x442;&#x438;&#x301;&#x43D;&#x43E;&#x432;&#x438;&#x447; &#x410;&#x439;&#x432;&#x430;&#x437;&#x43E;&#x301;&#x432;&#x441;&#x43A;&#x438;&#x439; (&#x201C;Ivan Konstantinovich Aivazovsky&#x201D;). Ivan is the given name, Konstantinovich the patronymic name, Aivazovsky the family name.</p></div>
<div class="example"><a name="_f028494e-29b7-476b-8544-145060d9515f" id="_f028494e-29b7-476b-8544-145060d9515f"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE  2</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#x41E;&#x43B;&#x435;&#x43A;&#x441;&#x430;&#x43D;&#x434;&#x440; &#x406;&#x432;&#x430;&#x43D;&#x43E;&#x432;&#x438;&#x447; &#x411;&#x456;&#x43B;&#x430;&#x448;, (&#x201C;Oleksandr Ivanovych Bilash&#x201D;).  Oleksandr is the given name, Ivanovych the patronymic name, Bilash the family name.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_slovenian" id="_slovenian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.40 Slovenian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_somali" id="_somali"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.41 Somali</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_spanish" id="_spanish"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.42 Spanish</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_d0f7f56d-82e2-46a2-94f4-e8d5bf9c0e66" id="_d0f7f56d-82e2-46a2-94f4-e8d5bf9c0e66"></a>given name(s) + father&#x2019;s patronymic name(s) + mother&#x2019;s patronymic name(s)</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_66d0097b-e379-4e5c-aef9-590ed35b0027" id="_66d0097b-e379-4e5c-aef9-590ed35b0027"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>Salvador de Madariaga y Rojo, Spanish diplomat, writer, historian and pacifist. Child of Dar&#xED;o Jos&#xE9; de Madariaga&#x200B; and Mar&#xED;a Ascensi&#xF3;n Rojo.</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_tamil" id="_tamil"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.43 Tamil</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_thai" id="_thai"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.44 Thai</p><p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_4ccce65a-1ec3-4451-a940-8870f3f05bca" id="_4ccce65a-1ec3-4451-a940-8870f3f05bca"></a>given name(s) + family name</p>
<p class="MsoNormal"><a name="_95790a00-b2ce-4974-afec-dbe059e1cdd9" id="_95790a00-b2ce-4974-afec-dbe059e1cdd9"></a>Modern Thai names are composed of given names followed by the family name.
Prior to 1913, family names were not required.</p>
<div class="example"><a name="_7ba4d0cc-4e2d-4e37-b656-01bcb253d27f" id="_7ba4d0cc-4e2d-4e37-b656-01bcb253d27f"></a><p class="MsoNormal"><span class="example_label">EXAMPLE</span><span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>&#xE0A;&#xE27;&#xE19; &#xE2B;&#xE25;&#xE35;&#xE01;&#xE20;&#xE31;&#xE22; (&#x201C;Chuan Likphai&#x201D;)</p></div></div>
        <div><a name="_turkish" id="_turkish"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.45 Turkish</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_ukrainian" id="_ukrainian"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.46 Ukrainian</p>

</div>
        <div><a name="_vietnamese" id="_vietnamese"></a><p class="h2Annex">B.47 Vietnamese</p>

</div>
      </div>
      <p class="MsoNormal">
        <br clear="all" style="mso-special-character:line-break;page-break-before:always"/>
      </p>
      <div>
        <h1 class="Section3">Bibliography</h1>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO9945" id="ISO9945"></a>[1]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO/IEC/IEEE 9945:2009, <i> Portable Operating System Interface (POSIX&#xAE;) Base Specifications, Issue 7</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO19788-5" id="ISO19788-5"></a>[2]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO/IEC 19788-5:2012, <i> Learning, education and training</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO12234-3" id="ISO12234-3"></a>[3]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO 12234-3:2016, <i> Removable memory</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO9075-1" id="ISO9075-1"></a>[4]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO/IEC 9075-1:2016, <i> Database languages</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO14813-5" id="ISO14813-5"></a>[5]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO 14813-5:2010, <i> Reference model architecture(s) for the ITS sector</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO14198" id="ISO14198"></a>[6]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO/TS 14198:2012, <i> Ergonomic aspects of transport information and control systems</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO29003" id="ISO29003"></a>[7]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO/IEC TS 29003:2018, <i> Security techniques</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO14105" id="ISO14105"></a>[8]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO/TR 14105:2011, <i> Change management for successful electronic document management system (EDMS) implementation</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO20944-1" id="ISO20944-1"></a>[9]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO/IEC 20944-1:2013, <i> Metadata Registries Interoperability and Bindings (MDR-IB)</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO5127" id="ISO5127"></a>[10]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO 5127:2017, <i> Foundation and vocabulary</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO31320" id="ISO31320"></a>[11]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO/IEC/IEEE 31320-2:2012, <i> Modeling Languages</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="iso639" id="iso639"></a>[12]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO 639, <i> Code for the representation of names of languages</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO639-3" id="ISO639-3"></a>[13]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO 639-3, <i> Codes for the representation of names of languages</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="ISO15961" id="ISO15961"></a>[14]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO/IEC 15961:2004, <i> Radio frequency identification (RFID) for item management</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="iso15924" id="iso15924"></a>[15]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>ISO 15924:2004, <i> Codes for the representation of names of scripts</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="rfc2046" id="rfc2046"></a>[16]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>IETF RFC 2046, <i>Multipurpose Internet Mail Extensions (MIME) Part Two: Media Types</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="rfc3986" id="rfc3986"></a>[17]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>IETF RFC 3986, <i>Uniform Resource Identifier (URI): Generic Syntax</i></p>
        <p class="Biblio"><a name="rfc8288" id="rfc8288"></a>[18]<span style="mso-tab-count:1">&#xA0; </span>IETF RFC 8288, <i>Web Linking</i></p>
      </div>
